in its very first round.\n\nThe terminal event on the run:\n\n```\nATTEMPT_PARKED  {\"code\":\"RECIPE_STOPPED\",\"message\":\"EXECUTION_FAILED: exit status 1\"}\n```\n\nThe sequence (run `ce428284`, attempt `9dc905dc`, task `T-20260914-chat-output`):\n\n| Time (UTC) | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 11:21:55 | `RUN_CREATED` → `LEASE_ACQUIRED` (fence 1) → `ROUTE_SELECTED` (adapter `chat-fixture`, supervised) |\n| 11:21:56 | `ADAPTER_PREPARING` — probe reports available, `managed_recipe_v1` capabilities present |\n| 11:21:59 | `RECIPE_ROUND_RESERVED` round 1, deadline 11:51:59 |\n| 11:22:01.400 | `RECIPE_ROUND_STARTED` round 1 |\n| 11:22:01.420 | `ATTEMPT_PARKED` — `EXECUTION_FAILED: exit status 1` |\n\nRound 1 lasted **15 milliseconds**. The root cause is the routed executable itself:\n\n```bash\n# .../stopped-claude-20260915T112144Z/fixture-worker.sh\nif [[ \"${1:-}\" == --version ]]; then echo chat-fixture/1; exit 0; fi\nexit 1\n```\n\nIt answers the capability probe (`--version`) successfully, which is why routing and preparation passed, then exits 1 unconditionally on the real work invocation. So the adapter never ran the recipe steps at all. The attempt artifact confirms it: `terminal_outcome: \"failed\"`, `output: \"\"`, `truncated: false` — no executor output, no eval evidence, only a `round-1` artifact.\n\nNo work product was produced. `a.txt` is 0 bytes in both the main checkout and the attempt worktree, and `b.txt` is 0 bytes. The task's done condition (`a.txt` == `completed`, 9 bytes; `b.txt` empty) is unmet, so evals 1 and 2 would fail — but note the evals never got to run; this is an execution failure, not an eval failure, and the two need different recovery decisions.\n\n## Remaining budget\n\nFrom `recipe_budgets` for revision `sha256:f4110c2d…`:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Rounds used | **1 of 3** (`max_rounds: 3`) → **2 remain** |\n| No-progress counter | **0** of 2 |\n| Signed deadline | **2026-09-15T11:51:59Z** — ~28 min left as of 11:23Z, and draining in real time |\n| Lease | `parked`, fence 1, expires 11:26:55Z |\n| Run state | `active` |\n| Task | `status=ready`, `signed_off=true`, `accepted=false` |\n\nThe budget is intact; it stopped with headroom on both rounds and clock. The parking was immediate and deliberate rather than a retry loop — the controller treated a non-zero executor exit as a blocker to report, not an evidenced failure to repair, so it did not burn rounds 2 and 3 against a binary that cannot run.\n\nOne caveat on that deadline: it is the signed total for this attempt, and it keeps expiring whether or not anything is running. Resuming would not refund rounds or restart that clock, so the window for reusing this attempt is largely gone by now regardless of the two unused rounds.\n\n## What I did and did not do\n\nAs instructed, I did not reset the budget, authorize a successor, or resume execution. I also avoided `mesh status`/`mesh watch`/`mesh explain` — their mutation contracts say existing-runtime inspection may restart the daemon or write Git metadata. The state above comes from a copy of `mesh.db` (plus WAL) read in `/tmp`, the on-disk attempt artifacts, and `taskspec status`, whose contract is `mutation: none`. Repository state is unchanged.\n\nThe blocker is environmental, not a defect in the task or its seal: the routed adapter executable cannot perform work. Whoever owns this run will need to fix or re-route the executor before any resume is meaningful — and given the deadline, that likely means reviewed successor work rather than reviving attempt `9dc905dc`. Both are your call, not mine to take.","ttft_ms":1768,"type":"result","duration_ms":117636,"uuid":"641e5d8e-24ed-4e2c-9874-026bd76e2721","ttft_stream_ms":1127,"time_to_request_ms":337,"first_content_frame_ms":1127,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
